,"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The  MIMO QFT design technique  provides  a  design procedure to synthesize a fixed diagonal controller transfer function matrix G (s) and prefilter F (s) to satisfy specifications  on  the  closed-loop  system  shown  in  Fig.2, where P (s) is the MIMO uncertain plant.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":681.4035899127275,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.06005658000004,"r":293.13320058,"t":736.38420488},"charspan":[0,270],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"The  MIMO QFT design technique  provides  a  design procedure to synthesize a fixed diagonal controller transfer function matrix G (s) and prefilter F (s) to satisfy specifications  on  the  closed-loop  system  shown  in  Fig.2, where P (s) is the MIMO uncertain plant."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The  basic  principle  for  MIMO  QFT  design  is  to convert the MIMO control system into a set of equivalent MISO  control  systems.  Using  fixed-point  theorem,  the MIMO  control  problem  for  an m  ×  m system  can  be converted  into m equivalent  single-loop  MISO  problems, each with two inputs and one output. The objective of the design  is  to  achieve  set  point  tracking,  while  minimizing the  outputs  due  to  the  disturbance  inputs  (cross-coupling effects) [5, 6].","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":576.1017722727279,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.06106020000006,"r":293.14524402000006,"t":677.8831950800002},"charspan":[0,490],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"The  basic  principle  for  MIMO  QFT  design  is  to convert the MIMO control system into a set of equivalent MISO  control  systems.  Using  fixed-point  theorem,  the MIMO  control  problem  for  an m  ×  m system  can  be converted  into m equivalent  single-loop  MISO  problems, each with two inputs and one output. The objective of the design  is  to  achieve  set  point  tracking,  while  minimizing the  outputs  due  to  the  disturbance  inputs  (cross-coupling effects) [5, 6]."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"V.  COMBINED QFT/EEAS DESIGN","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":558.4019299527279,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":101.28120138000008,"r":242.86905000000002,"t":566.5840472},"charspan":[0,28],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"V.  COMBINED QFT/EEAS DESIGN"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"A. Problem formulation","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":534.7037537727274,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.060206460000046,"r":155.3764692600001,"t":542.8835609},"charspan":[0,22],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"A. Problem formulation"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"With  no  loss  of  generality  and  for  simplicity,  the design process is developed for 2 × 2 MIMO plants. The procedure  can  be  easily  extended  to  the  general  MIMO case. Consider the feedback structure shown in Fig. 3. The transfer function matrix 1,2 , , )] ( [ ) ( = = j i s p s P ij represents  the  LTI  uncertain  2  ×  2  MIMO  plant  to  be controlled. The 1,2,3 )], ( ), ( [ 2 1 = = i s g s g diag G i i i and )] ( ), ( [ ) ( 22 11 s f s f diag s F = ,  which  are  assumed  diagonal, represent  the  feedforward  compensators  and  the  prefilter matrix,  respectively.  Also,  the  nonlinear  elements 1 N and 2 N are assumed to be ideal relays with outputs 01 M ± and 02 M ± . Moreover, 1 V and 2 V are the excitation signals.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":373.25053883636366,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.06006527999999,"r":293.1965785200001,"t":531.1833589400001},"charspan":[0,748],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"With  no  loss  of  generality  and  for  simplicity,  the design process is developed for 2 × 2 MIMO plants. The procedure  can  be  easily  extended  to  the  general  MIMO case. Consider the feedback structure shown in Fig. 3. The transfer function matrix 1,2 , , )] ( [ ) ( = = j i s p s P ij represents  the  LTI  uncertain  2  ×  2  MIMO  plant  to  be controlled. The 1,2,3 )], ( ), ( [ 2 1 = = i s g s g diag G i i i and )] ( ), ( [ ) ( 22 11 s f s f diag s F = ,  which  are  assumed  diagonal, represent  the  feedforward  compensators  and  the  prefilter matrix,  respectively.  Also,  the  nonlinear  elements 1 N and 2 N are assumed to be ideal relays with outputs 01 M ± and 02 M ± . Moreover, 1 V and 2 V are the excitation signals."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Let ) ( / s T R Y be  the  input-output  relation  from  the input ) ( s R to the output ) ( s Y , which is clearly derived as","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":345.22413555272726,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.05978442,"r":293.1178741200002,"t":368.4040472},"charspan":[0,126],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Let ) ( / s T R Y be  the  input-output  relation  from  the input ) ( s R to the output ) ( s Y , which is clearly derived as"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":") ( ) ( ) ( ) ] ( ) ( [ ) ( 1 / s P s G s F s P s G I s T R Y -+ = (8)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":318.98727910303023,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":52.262359060000044,"r":294.04865897999997,"t":332.95284877999995},"charspan":[0,70],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"where, 1 2 3 G G N G G f = ( ) , ( 2 1 f f f n n diag N = , are the describing functions of the relays).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":281.68394085272735,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.06,"r":293.08839749999987,"t":309.8108},"charspan":[0,104],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"where, 1 2 3 G G N G G f = ( ) , ( 2 1 f f f n n diag N = , are the describing functions of the relays)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Due to uncertainty, P ∈ {P} is a set of possible plants and it is assumed here that the plant set is finite or can be adequately approximated by a finite set so that numerical algorithms  can  be  developed.  The  combined  QFT/EEAS control design task is to find ) ( s Gi and ) ( s F with proper rational and stable elements, in order to satisfy the performance specifications ∀ P ∈ {P}. For example, tracking specifications may require that ∀ P ∈ {P}, where, ) ( ω ij A and ) ( ω ij B are the upper and lower specifications.  For  simplicity,  this  paper  will  concentrate on  tracking  performance  in  (9),  but  there  may  be  other specifications on sensitivity, sensor noise to input sensitivity,  as  well  as  engineering  considerations  such  as those in direct  MIMO-QFT.  At  high  frequencies  the benefits of feedback are negligible. High frequency specifications will result in large bandwidth with very little closed-loop performance improvement. It is thus recommended that the specifications to be enforced to the lowest possible frequency h ω (the Horowitz frequency). In addition, an implicit design objective is the minimization of the  loop  bandwidths  when  sensor  noise  attenuation  is concerned.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":181.46443999999997,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":51.05955486000002,"r":293.13872058000015,"t":281.04050000000007},"charspan":[0,453],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":473.92322129696976,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":304.74,"r":546.8798152000012,"t":644.752925},"charspan":[454,1227],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Due to uncertainty, P ∈ {P} is a set of possible plants and it is assumed here that the plant set is finite or can be adequately approximated by a finite set so that numerical algorithms  can  be  developed.  The  combined  QFT/EEAS control design task is to find ) ( s Gi and ) ( s F with proper rational and stable elements, in order to satisfy the performance specifications ∀ P ∈ {P}. For example, tracking specifications may require that ∀ P ∈ {P}, where, ) ( ω ij A and ) ( ω ij B are the upper and lower specifications.  For  simplicity,  this  paper  will  concentrate on  tracking  performance  in  (9),  but  there  may  be  other specifications on sensitivity, sensor noise to input sensitivity,  as  well  as  engineering  considerations  such  as those in direct  MIMO-QFT.  At  high  frequencies  the benefits of feedback are negligible. High frequency specifications will result in large bandwidth with very little closed-loop performance improvement. It is thus recommended that the specifications to be enforced to the lowest possible frequency h ω (the Horowitz frequency). In addition, an implicit design objective is the minimization of the  loop  bandwidths  when  sensor  noise  attenuation  is concerned."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Fig. 2.  2DOF MIMO-QFT Control Structure","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":82.03123818181814,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":97.26,"r":243.8206799999998,"t":88.53517999999997},"charspan":[0,40],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"Fig. 2.  2DOF MIMO-QFT Control Structure"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":") ( ) ( ) ( / ω ω ω ij ij R Y ij A j T B ≤ ≤ (9)","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":158.84232864121213,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":53.21985400000001,"r":294.0491064,"t":174.14272000000005},"charspan":[0,48],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":") ( ) ( ) ( / ω ω ω ij ij R Y ij A j T B ≤ ≤ (9)"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"F","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":127.52959241212125,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":81.06,"r":85.6410947,"t":133.63196879999998},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"F"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(s)","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"b